  "equal_range_semantics": "tsl::hopscotch_map is a UNIQUE-key (std::unordered_map-like) container, so equal_range degenerates to a \"0-or-1 element\" range, exactly like std::unordered_map::equal_range.\n\nContract, expressed in terms of find()/end():\n  auto it = find(key);\n  if (it == end()) return {end(), end()};   // key ABSENT -> empty range\n  else             return {it, std::next(it)}; // key PRESENT -> half-open range covering exactly that one element\n\n- Key PRESENT: range.first points at the single element whose key compares equal; range.second is the immediate successor position in iteration order (which is end() when the found element happens to be last in iteration order). Therefore std::distance(range.first, range.second) == 1 and the counting loop `for (it = first; it != second; ++it) num_in_range++` executes its body exactly once -> yields 1.\n- Key ABSENT: range.first == range.second (both end(), or more generally two equal iterators), the loop condition `it != second` is false on entry -> yields 0.\n- Consequence: num_in_range is always identical to map.count(key), i.e. it is a boolean-valued 0/1 presence test. Multiplicity can never exceed 1 because insert() on a duplicate key is a no-op that returns {existing_iterator, false}. So the \"range\" concept from multimaps (where equal_range can span N>1 equal keys) never produces N>1 here. This is the whole point of the test: it pins equal_range to unique-key semantics.\n- Bucket-order caveat that does NOT affect the count: hopscotch hashing means iteration order is unspecified/implementation-defined, so WHICH element range.second names varies with count and hash layout, but the DISTANCE is invariantly 1. A faithful port must not try to reproduce C++ iteration order; it only has to reproduce distance == 1 (present) / 0 (absent).",
