scriminator is emptiness of the map, i.e. sign/zero-ness of count. There is no count >= 1 that prints anything other than 1, and no count <= 0 that prints anything other than 0. A correct Rust port therefore prints exactly two possible values, 0 and 1.\n\nArgument-parsing behavior the port must also match (observed):\n- No args, or argv[1] != \"--a\" (e.g. `--b 7`): count stays 15 -> prints 1, exit 0.\n- `--a <n>`: count = stoi(n).\n- `--a` with NO argv[2]: C++ constructs std::string from a NULL pointer -> throws std::logic_error \"basic_string::_S_construct null not valid\" -> abort, exit 134, nothing printed on stdout. (Rust: this is a panic/abort path, not a 0.)\n- `--a abc` (no leading digits): std::stoi throws std::invalid_argument -> abort, exit 134.\n- `--a 9xyz`: std::stoi parses the leading digits and IGNORES the trailing junk -> count = 9 -> prints 1, exit 0. A naive Rust `str::parse::<i32>()` would FAIL here, so the port needs stoi-style prefix parsing to be faithful.",
